Whee! Ed? Oh, look, Jason, I was just starting to like you, so don't go saying

254
00:15:37,680 --> 00:15:40,320
something that's gonna make me have no use for you again, okay?

255
00:15:40,900 --> 00:15:43,060
Well, this could be a short conversation.

256
00:15:49,660 --> 00:15:54,420
Perfect. Ed, I could use your help.

257
00:15:55,040 --> 00:16:00,060
Car trouble? No, you see, uh, well, right now I'm having a problem keeping Maggie

258
00:16:00,060 --> 00:16:03,060
happy. Here and now I won't put up with any of that sex talk.

259
00:16:04,080 --> 00:16:05,220
No, it's nothing like that, Ed.

260
00:16:05,220 --> 00:16:08,600
It's just, well, she asked me to come out here to tell you that this whole idea of

261
00:16:08,600 --> 00:16:11,820
you spending the rest of your life traveling is stupid.

262
00:16:13,100 --> 00:16:14,880
My problem is I think it's fabulous.

263
00:16:15,900 --> 00:16:18,840
And she also wanted me to tell you that you're treating Kate unfairly.

264
00:16:18,840 --> 00:16:21,860
I don't know if I agree with that or not, but I do know it's none of my business.

265
00:16:21,980 --> 00:16:23,180
Well, you're right about that.

266
00:16:24,060 --> 00:16:26,100
Well, I have to go tell her that we talked about something.

267
00:16:26,380 --> 00:16:32,200
Like what? Well, like, uh, what am I supposed to say to her when she says, how come

268
00:16:32,200 --> 00:16:34,300
my dad did all this without consulting my mom?

269
00:16:35,020 --> 00:16:36,920
Why should I have to consult her?

270
00:16:38,420 --> 00:16:41,340
Well, and some married couples actually do talk to each other.

271
00:16:41,500 --> 00:16:45,340
You're talking about that Phil Donahue and Marlon Perkins, ain't you?

272
00:16:46,820 --> 00:16:50,000
Now, could you at least tell me why you suddenly sold your home?

273
00:16:51,820 --> 00:16:56,180
Why? I don't know why.

274
00:16:57,700 --> 00:16:59,240
Maybe I ran out of chores.

275
00:16:59,780 --> 00:17:02,560
Replanted the rose garden, extended the back porch, painted.

276
00:17:02,660 --> 00:17:03,360
I did those things.

277
00:17:03,500 --> 00:17:05,060
The first six months I was on retirement.

278
00:17:05,260 --> 00:17:06,880
I don't know why.

279
00:17:07,560 --> 00:17:11,780
Just recently the leg broke off of my old easy chair because I was spending so much

280
00:17:11,780 --> 00:17:14,140
sitting in it watching that wheel of fortune.

281
00:17:14,580 --> 00:17:16,600
Which is not a half bad show, by the way.

282
00:17:18,680 --> 00:17:24,200
Maybe it's because last month we paid the house off after 25 years and not missing a

283
00:17:24,200 --> 00:17:28,180
single payment. And to celebrate, we went home and fell asleep.

284
00:17:29,960 --> 00:17:31,760
I don't know why.

285
00:17:32,840 --> 00:17:34,380
Let me ask you something.

286
00:17:36,060 --> 00:17:38,380
How many years you figure you got left?

287
00:17:40,080 --> 00:17:41,800
I haven't thought about it much.

288
00:17:43,100 --> 00:17:44,940
I do. A lot.

289
00:17:46,620 --> 00:17:50,120
I understand. You do that?

290
00:17:50,540 --> 00:17:53,200
Yeah. Oh, I wish my wife did.

291
00:17:53,560 --> 00:17:55,460
Well, have you tried telling her how you feel?

292
00:17:55,820 --> 00:17:59,200
Look, if she wanted to marry a communicator, she should have married Walter

293
00:17:59,200 --> 00:18:02,200
Cronkite. Ha! She's gonna change her mind.

294
00:18:04,060 --> 00:18:06,860
Won't she? He'll change his mind.

295
00:18:06,860 --> 00:18:10,040
Won't he? Sure he will, Mom.

296
00:18:10,040 --> 00:18:11,900
He's a sensitive, giving human being.

297
00:18:11,980 --> 00:18:12,920
He's a jackass.

298
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,900
You're right. Maggie, he's your father.

299
00:18:17,560 --> 00:18:21,040
Psst. What will I do with myself?

300
00:18:21,640 --> 00:18:24,280
Oh, Mom, I'm sure that everything will work out for the best.

301
00:18:24,780 --> 00:18:28,940
I know. I'll do what you did, resume my career.

302
00:18:30,220 --> 00:18:33,260
Mom, you were a riveter during World War II.

303
00:18:36,120 --> 00:18:37,600
Oh, honey, how did it go?

304
00:18:38,040 --> 00:18:38,640
How did it go?

305
00:18:38,900 --> 00:18:40,380
Oh, it went well.

306
00:18:40,720 --> 00:18:42,040
Actually, I think it went very well.

307
00:18:43,260 --> 00:18:44,460
Very well, indeed.

308
00:18:49,180 --> 00:18:56,840
Eddie! Daddy? Daddy?

309
00:18:57,720 --> 00:18:59,580
This has gone far enough!

310
00:19:00,180 --> 00:19:02,900
This is no business of yours, Margaret Catherine.

311
00:19:02,900 --> 00:19:05,400
Do you have anything to say, Kate?

312
00:19:05,820 --> 00:19:08,080
No. Do you have anything to say?

313
00:19:08,560 --> 00:19:09,640
Not a word.

314
00:19:09,820 --> 00:19:10,960
All right, then.

315
00:19:11,020 --> 00:19:11,840
Hold it, Mom.

316
00:19:12,320 --> 00:19:15,380
Now, if you're not gonna speak for yourself, then I am gonna speak for you.

317
00:19:15,460 --> 00:19:17,060
Meggie, you shouldn't speak for somebody else.

318
00:19:17,180 --> 00:19:19,820
Well, somebody sure has to with the screwy way this man is behaving.

319
00:19:20,060 --> 00:19:21,780
Your father hasn't done anything screwy.

320
00:19:21,900 --> 00:19:23,440
Oh, come on, Jason.

321
00:19:23,500 --> 00:19:26,820
Just the whole idea of spending the rest of their lives in this thing?

322
00:19:27,080 --> 00:19:28,080
It's way out there.

323
00:19:29,640 --> 00:19:32,200
Meggie, this happens to be a dream for some people.

324
00:19:32,200 --> 00:19:34,900
A lot of intelligent, responsible people, I might add.

325
00:19:35,140 --> 00:19:36,440
Well, name one.

326
00:19:37,060 --> 00:19:41,640
Me. I'd love to spend the rest of my days like this.

327
00:19:42,840 --> 00:19:44,180
What is it with you men?

328
00:19:44,380 --> 00:19:46,060
What do you have against houses?

329
00:19:47,220 --> 00:19:49,620
Well, your father might have been acting a little impulsively.

330
00:19:49,760 --> 00:19:51,140
Maggie, but he was a desperate man.

331
00:19:51,340 --> 00:19:53,940
He had nothing to look forward to except...

332
00:19:53,940 --> 00:19:56,200
Wheel of Fortune, which is not a half -bad show.

333
00:19:57,920 --> 00:19:59,840
Well, then why didn't he tell Mom that?

334
00:19:59,920 --> 00:20:01,000
Because that's not his style.

335
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:02,340
Well, his style stinks.

336
00:20:03,500 --> 00:20:05,380
His style is fine for 37 years.

337
00:20:05,500 --> 00:20:06,940
38. Mom, do you mind?

338
00:20:06,980 --> 00:20:08,220
We're having a discussion here.

339
00:20:09,080 --> 00:20:11,540
Margaret Catherine, I won't have you talking to your mother that way.

340
00:20:12,140 --> 00:20:15,320
Well, Dad, at least I talk to her.

341
00:20:15,660 --> 00:20:18,340
I can talk to your mother as well as anyone can.

342
00:20:19,020 --> 00:20:22,140
Kate! Oh, you're all crisp up.

343
00:20:22,540 --> 00:20:25,300
I'm sorry. I should have told you I was selling the house.

344
00:20:25,420 --> 00:20:27,460
See, he said told instead of asked.

345
00:20:28,020 --> 00:20:31,540
Eddie, I didn't know you were so unhappy at home.

346
00:20:32,380 --> 00:20:34,480
Well, you know I don't complain much.

347
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:38,260
Look, the house isn't gone through escrow yet.

348
00:20:38,620 --> 00:20:42,340
I can take this thing back to Smiling Jake's RV and get us our money back.

349
00:20:42,500 --> 00:20:45,700
Eddie, are you asking me what I want to do?

350
00:20:46,940 --> 00:20:48,340
Well, kind of, yeah.

351
00:20:50,280 --> 00:20:56,300
Well, what would make you happy is what would make me happy.

352
00:20:59,800 --> 00:21:06,340
Oh. Oh, what are we standing out here for?

353
00:21:06,540 --> 00:21:09,400
There's a whole big world out there to see.

354
00:21:09,520 --> 00:21:11,520
Well, you're right about that, Kate.

355
00:21:11,700 --> 00:21:13,780
Maggie, will you tell the kids we said goodbye?

356
00:21:14,040 --> 00:21:17,380
And it was sure nice talking to you, uh, uh, Jason.

357
00:21:18,180 --> 00:21:22,880
Right. Mom, what about your suitcase?

358
00:21:23,600 --> 00:21:27,280
Send it to me in care of general delivery in Philadelphia.

359
00:21:27,280 --> 00:21:31,600
Kate, I was thinking we should go back to Niagara Falls.

360
00:21:31,680 --> 00:21:32,460
I mean, if it's okay.

361
00:21:33,420 --> 00:21:35,280
It's better than okay.

362
00:21:40,600 --> 00:21:42,920
Maggie, never mind.

363
00:21:43,160 --> 00:21:44,980
I won't be needing my clothes.

364
00:21:45,300 --> 00:21:48,340
Stand back! Oh, my God.

365
00:21:55,240 --> 00:21:57,380
Oh, Jason, isn't it wonderful?

366
00:21:57,780 --> 00:22:02,740
Yeah. And all that baloney you were spouting how you'd really like to live like that

367
00:22:02,740 --> 00:22:03,940
did the trick.

368
00:22:06,360 --> 00:22:08,120
Well, I am a professional.

369
00:22:10,520 --> 00:22:11,620
I'm sorry. Ah!

370
00:22:17,020 --> 00:22:20,900
I gouged! I gouged!

371
00:22:23,840 --> 00:22:26,440
Mom, my dad, don't worry, it's just me.

372
00:22:38,120 --> 00:22:39,880
This way, Mike.

373
00:22:40,660 --> 00:22:42,370
It worked. He's following me.

374
00:22:49,800 --> 00:22:51,320
Where are you?

375
00:22:51,760 --> 00:22:53,520
Over here, Mike.

376
00:22:54,720 --> 00:22:57,680
No, Mike. I said over here.

377
00:22:58,880 --> 00:22:59,740
Where are you?

378
00:22:59,740 --> 00:23:01,140
I can't see you guys.

379
00:23:02,220 --> 00:23:03,040
Where you going?

380
00:23:03,520 --> 00:23:38,100
Now! Oh, my God!
